\newlabel{sec:s3}{{}{29}{Supplementary Material S3: Analyses, Comparators, and the Case Study of the Audited Pipeline}{section*.41}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S3.1}{\ignorespaces Hyperparameters and computing environment.\relax }}{29}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:hiper}{{S3.1}{29}{Hyperparameters and computing environment.\relax }{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S3.2}{\ignorespaces Feature-shuffle test (null experiment; Hamdan et al.) on the C1 and I2 pipelines without age and sex (50 canonical folds; 100 replicates per scenario). Pooled AUC in percent with the 5th--95th percentile across replicates; the last columns are the AUC within era strata as a validity diagnostic. Rule set beforehand: a mean I2 up to 52 means no evidence of leakage, 52--55 slight exploitation (the category later named ``uncertain''), and above 55 evidence of leakage.\relax }}{29}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:hamdan}{{S3.2}{29}{Feature-shuffle test (null experiment; Hamdan et al.) on the C1 and I2 pipelines without age and sex (50 canonical folds; 100 replicates per scenario). Pooled AUC in percent with the 5th--95th percentile across replicates; the last columns are the AUC within era strata as a validity diagnostic. Rule set beforehand: a mean I2 up to 52 means no evidence of leakage, 52--55 slight exploitation (the category later named ``uncertain''), and above 55 evidence of leakage.\relax }{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S3.3}{\ignorespaces Sensitivity and feature-shuffle test of the covariate adjustment on I2, P3, and NM (50 canonical folds). The H1 and H2a columns are the mean pooled AUC (percent) on uninformative features (without age and sex, with 100 replicates for I2 and P3 and, for NM, a reduced version with 20 replicates, repetition 0 only, one configuration, and the H1 scenario). Rule set beforehand: up to 52 no leakage, 52--55 slight exploitation (later ``uncertain''), above 55 leakage, and for NM additionally the 95th percentile of the mean up to 54. The next two columns are the per-fold AUC on real data without and with age and sex (Nadeau--Bengio 95\% CI). The AUC within year and device strata was computed for the variants with age and sex; the metadata baseline reached 43.2. V4 was added after the dry run and before the full run; I2--V0 is the I2 pipeline with the control-referenced adjustment originally planned; V4 and V5 (the counterpart of V4 for P3) are covariate regression on all training patients.\relax }}{30}{table.caption.44}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sensitivitas}{{S3.3}{30}{Sensitivity and feature-shuffle test of the covariate adjustment on I2, P3, and NM (50 canonical folds). The H1 and H2a columns are the mean pooled AUC (percent) on uninformative features (without age and sex, with 100 replicates for I2 and P3 and, for NM, a reduced version with 20 replicates, repetition 0 only, one configuration, and the H1 scenario). Rule set beforehand: up to 52 no leakage, 52--55 slight exploitation (later ``uncertain''), above 55 leakage, and for NM additionally the 95th percentile of the mean up to 54. The next two columns are the per-fold AUC on real data without and with age and sex (Nadeau--Bengio 95\% CI). The AUC within year and device strata was computed for the variants with age and sex; the metadata baseline reached 43.2. V4 was added after the dry run and before the full run; I2--V0 is the I2 pipeline with the control-referenced adjustment originally planned; V4 and V5 (the counterpart of V4 for P3) are covariate regression on all training patients.\relax }{table.caption.44}{}}
\newlabel{tab:kebocoran}{{S3.3}{30}{Sensitivity and feature-shuffle test of the covariate adjustment on I2, P3, and NM (50 canonical folds). The H1 and H2a columns are the mean pooled AUC (percent) on uninformative features (without age and sex, with 100 replicates for I2 and P3 and, for NM, a reduced version with 20 replicates, repetition 0 only, one configuration, and the H1 scenario). Rule set beforehand: up to 52 no leakage, 52--55 slight exploitation (later ``uncertain''), above 55 leakage, and for NM additionally the 95th percentile of the mean up to 54. The next two columns are the per-fold AUC on real data without and with age and sex (Nadeau--Bengio 95\% CI). The AUC within year and device strata was computed for the variants with age and sex; the metadata baseline reached 43.2. V4 was added after the dry run and before the full run; I2--V0 is the I2 pipeline with the control-referenced adjustment originally planned; V4 and V5 (the counterpart of V4 for P3) are covariate regression on all training patients.\relax }{table.caption.44}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S3.4}{\ignorespaces Skewness and kurtosis of the raw and transformed features (before covariate adjustment), computed per feature in double precision over all observation units of each representation. Kurtosis is Fisher excess kurtosis ($0$ for a normal distribution); the columns give the median and the maximum over features, and the last column the largest absolute feature value. Each reproduced method used its own segmentation: Rohira 10 s windows over the whole recording without artifact rejection (28,982 windows, the main pipeline has 27,596 windows before and 26,968 after rejection), Tang 4,702 sequences of 60 one-second steps from 199 patients (282,120 seconds), and NM 4 s segments (66,106). Symlog is $\mathrm  {sign}(x)\qopname  \relax o{ln}(1+|x|)$; the Rohira log pipeline used $\qopname  \relax o{ln}x$. The values were recomputed by script in round 49 (analysis 49F); the earlier version of this table reported the largest absolute Tang value ($8\times 10^{8}$) as a kurtosis and computed the Tang and NM rows on subsamples. The Rohira symlog and rank-normal rows and the Emotiv rows (5,987 windows of 299 subjects, 60 band-power features) were added later with the transformations of Eq.~(\ref  {eq:transformasi}). Rank-normal was fitted on all units because the table is descriptive.\relax }}{30}{table.caption.46}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:kurtosis}{{S3.4}{30}{Skewness and kurtosis of the raw and transformed features (before covariate adjustment), computed per feature in double precision over all observation units of each representation. Kurtosis is Fisher excess kurtosis ($0$ for a normal distribution); the columns give the median and the maximum over features, and the last column the largest absolute feature value. Each reproduced method used its own segmentation: Rohira 10 s windows over the whole recording without artifact rejection (28,982 windows, the main pipeline has 27,596 windows before and 26,968 after rejection), Tang 4,702 sequences of 60 one-second steps from 199 patients (282,120 seconds), and NM 4 s segments (66,106). Symlog is $\mathrm {sign}(x)\ln (1+|x|)$; the Rohira log pipeline used $\ln x$. The values were recomputed by script in round 49 (analysis 49F); the earlier version of this table reported the largest absolute Tang value ($8\times 10^{8}$) as a kurtosis and computed the Tang and NM rows on subsamples. The Rohira symlog and rank-normal rows and the Emotiv rows (5,987 windows of 299 subjects, 60 band-power features) were added later with the transformations of \eqref {eq:transformasi}. Rank-normal was fitted on all units because the table is descriptive.\relax }{table.caption.46}{}}
